When a small group of organisms (a small population) are separated from their main population (e.g. if they move across a mountain range or river, or move to a new island so that they can't return easily), they'll often find themselves in an environment different from the environment in which they were.
Given that this new environment has different resources and competitors, the new group will need different characteristics or adaptations to become a stronger competitor compared to what it previously needed.
There has been no change in the indigenous people at all, they still need the same methods of adaptation as usual.
Over time, as new residents begin to adapt to their new environment, their similarity with other populations gradually decreases.
In the end, after thousands or even millions of years, the two groups will look so different that we can’t call them the same species.
We call this process the process of speciation, which simply means the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable consequence and a very important part of evolution.
