Technological determinism is a term that encompasses a wide range of ideas in practice, from pushing technology or technological necessity to a strict sense that man’s fate is driven by a fundamental logic linked to scientific laws and their manifestations in technology.
There are two common ideas common to most interpretations of technological determinism: that the development of technology itself follows a path that goes far beyond cultural or political influence, and that this technology in turn has “influences” on deep-rooted societies, rather than those associated with society.
For example, one might say that a car engine necessarily leads to road development.
But the creation of a nationwide road network is not economically feasible for a handful of cars, so new roads are being developed to reduce the cost of owning a car.
Collective ownership of cars also leads to a high rate of road accidents, leading to the creation of new technologies in health care to treat damaged objects.
